<BaseLayout title="Pre-treatment estimates — Dana" description="Making insurance estimates easier to find, submit, and track for Canada Life plan members.">
  <header class="case-hero"><div class="container"><a class="back" href="/#work">← Selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">Canada Life · Released product</p><h1>Making pre-treatment estimates easier to find and track</h1><p class="lead">A research-led redesign that gave plan members a clear path to understand expected coverage before committing to care.</p></div></header>

  <section class="snapshot"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Project snapshot</p><h2>Making a hidden, time-sensitive service visible</h2><p>Requesting an estimate—also called a predetermination—was buried inside the claim experience. The work created a dedicated path for submitting and tracking estimates across responsive web.</p></div><dl><div><dt>My role</dt><dd>Lead UX Designer</dd></div><div><dt>Users</dt><dd>Group-benefits members planning healthcare expenses</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Discovery synthesis, information architecture, flows, UI, handoff, and design QA</dd></div><div><dt>Partners</dt><dd>Product, research, content, business analysis, engineering, accessibility, and QA</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Released in the Canada Life group-benefits portal</dd></div></dl></div></section>

  <section><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p><h2>Members could not find the service when they needed it</h2><p class="large">Before an important procedure, members needed to understand what their plan might cover. But the estimate option was embedded inside other claim categories, terminology was inconsistent, and the tracking path was unclear.</p><div class="challenge"><strong>Design challenge</strong><p>How might we make estimates and predeterminations easy to find, submit, and track—without requiring members to understand the organization’s internal terminology?</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="tinted"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p><h2>Three sources revealed a findability problem</h2><p>The redesign brought together behavioural data, information-architecture research, and unstructured customer feedback.</p></div><div class="evidence-grid"><article><strong>25</strong><h3>Tree-test participants</h3><p>For estimate submission, 10 chose Make a claim and 9 chose Benefits. For tracking, 16 chose Benefits and 6 chose Overview—evidence that one hidden path would not match everyone’s mental model.</p></article><article><strong>24,177</strong><h3>Recorded submissions</h3><p>Adobe Analytics recorded 20,032 English and 4,145 French estimate submissions over a 13-month period, demonstrating meaningful demand for the service.</p></article><article><strong>1,476</strong><h3>Qualtrics comments reviewed</h3><p>Recurring themes included difficulty finding estimates, unclear terminology, and uncertainty about where to track a submitted request.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="flows"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Information architecture</p><h2>From a concealed route to deliberate entry points</h2><p>The flow redesign separated estimates from unrelated claim categories and added access from locations supported by the tree-test findings.</p></div><div class="flow-compare"><figure><a href="/images/estimates/old-flow.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/estimates/old-flow.png" alt="Old estimate submission flow showing a long path through claim categories" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>Before · Embedded path</b><span>Members had to infer that an estimate lived inside another claim journey.</span></figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/images/estimates/new-flow.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/estimates/new-flow.png" alt="New estimate submission flow with dedicated entry points and a shorter route" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>After · Dedicated path</b><span>A distinct landing page and multiple entry points made the service visible.</span></figcaption></figure></div><div class="link-row"><a class="text-link" href={oldPrototype} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Explore the previous flow in Figma ↗</a><a class="text-link" href={newPrototype} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Explore the redesigned flow in Figma ↗</a></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Design exploration</p><h2>Testing how the estimate entry point should communicate</h2><p>Multiple combinations of content, controls, and component patterns were explored before the team selected a direction that balanced clarity with Salesforce component constraints.</p></div><figure class="wide-artifact"><a href="/images/estimates/component-iterations.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/estimates/component-iterations.png" alt="A collection of estimate start-page component and content variations" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>Component and content iterations</b><span>Alternatives explored hierarchy, selection patterns, and the amount of guidance shown before starting.</span></figcaption></figure></div></section>

  <section class="tinted"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Design decisions</p><h2>Four changes made the service easier to understand</h2><div class="decisions"><article><span>01</span><h3>Use both “estimate” and “predetermination”</h3><p>Customer language and insurance terminology appear together, reducing the burden of knowing the official term.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Create more than one credible entry point</h3><p>Members can enter from Make a claim and the left navigation, reflecting the different locations revealed through tree testing.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Give estimates a dedicated landing page</h3><p>Required documents, starting the request, and tracking prior submissions are gathered into one recognizable destination.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Reduce the primary journey</h3><p>The redesigned structure reduced the documented flow from seven steps to four while preserving necessary insurance information.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container grid responsive"><div><p class="eyebrow">Responsive experience</p><h2>One hierarchy across desktop and mobile</h2><p>The key actions—understanding requirements, starting an estimate, and finding submitted estimates—remain prominent across viewport sizes.</p><p>The interface also had to work within Salesforce Industries and Lightning Web Component constraints, so design decisions were reviewed with technical partners throughout delivery.</p></div><div class="mockups"><img src="/images/estimates/desktop-entry.png" alt="Desktop mockup of the released estimate entry experience" loading="lazy"/><img src="/images/estimates/mobile-entry.png" alt="Mobile mockup of the estimate entry experience" loading="lazy"/></div></div></section>

  <section class="prototype"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Experience in motion</p><h2>Web and mobile walkthroughs</h2><p>These recordings show the final flow across both responsive contexts.</p></div><div class="videos"><article><div class="video-frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/L02cehfR18A" title="Estimate experience, web view" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><h3>Web view</h3></article><article><div class="video-frame mobile-video"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/KZ-hdAeZ4_U" title="Estimate experience, mobile view" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><h3>Mobile view</h3></article></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Delivery</p><h2>Carried from requirements through implementation</h2><ol class="delivery"><li><b>1</b><strong>Frame the requirement</strong><small>Review priorities and constraints with product and business analysis.</small></li><li><b>2</b><strong>Synthesize evidence</strong><small>Connect research, analytics, feedback, and feasibility.</small></li><li><b>3</b><strong>Explore and align</strong><small>Iterate in Figma and review the direction with partners.</small></li><li><b>4</b><strong>Support delivery</strong><small>Handoff, design reviews, accessibility review, QA, and end-to-end testing.</small></li></ol></div></section>

  <section class="impact"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Outcome</p><h2>A distinct, released estimate experience</h2></div><div class="impact-grid"><article><strong>7 → 4</strong><p>documented reduction in steps</p></article><article><strong>2 paths</strong><p>primary entry points aligned with research</p></article><article><strong>Released</strong><p>responsive experience delivered to production</p></article></div><p class="caveat">The source material does not include verified post-launch call-volume or satisfaction measurements, so those are not presented as quantified outcomes.</p></div></section>

  <section class="dark"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Reflection</p><h2>Findability is part of the product—not a layer around it</h2></div><div><p>A well-designed form cannot help members who never find it. This project showed why navigation, language, and tracking must be designed as part of the service itself.</p><p>Using several evidence sources also prevented one entry point from being treated as universally “obvious.” The final structure acknowledges that members approach the same task with different mental models.</p></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">Next project</p><h2>Continue exploring the portfolio</h2><p class="large">Return to selected work to review the medical-claims redesign or the upcoming account-management case study.</p><a class="button" href="/#work">Return to selected work</a></div></section>

